        <h2>What is the difference between HubSpot Professional and Enterprise?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">For CRM architecture, Enterprise mainly adds structure and control: custom objects for data that does not fit contacts, companies, deals or tickets, a sandbox for testing changes safely, finer access controls, and higher limits for larger teams. Professional covers most B2B sales and marketing processes well when the portal is designed properly.</p>
        <p>This is not a pricing page; HubSpot changes plans and prices often, so check the current details on <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/pricing" rel="noopener">HubSpot's pricing page</a>. It is the view from building portals on both tiers: which differences change how you design the CRM, and which you can safely ignore.</p>

        <section id="objects">
          <h2>Do you need custom objects?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Custom objects are an Enterprise feature. You need them when your business tracks something with its own records, properties and relationships, such as properties, vehicles, subscriptions or projects, that would otherwise be crammed into deals or contacts. If a few properties on an existing object model it cleanly, Professional is enough.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Ask: does this thing have its own lifecycle and many records per customer?</li>
            <li>If yes, a custom object keeps reporting clean.</li>
            <li>If no, properties or an extra deal pipeline are usually the simpler choice.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="sandbox">
          <h2>How important is a sandbox?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">HubSpot includes a sandbox account with Enterprise, so changes to workflows, properties and pipelines can be built and tested away from live data, then deployed. On Professional you can still test safely with sample records and careful staging, but the risk of a change hitting live records is higher.</p>
          <p>The more automation and integrations a portal runs, the more a sandbox is worth. That is the same reason we build in a test environment first on <a href="/resources/hubspot-implementation-checklist/">every implementation</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="permissions">
          <h2>What changes for permissions and governance?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Both tiers support teams, permission sets and controls over who can view or edit specific properties. Enterprise adds finer control as organisations grow: more structured team hierarchies and stricter control over who can change what. If several departments or regions share one portal, those controls start to matter.</p>
          <p>Whichever tier you are on, the basics in our <a href="/resources/hubspot-governance-checklist-pipelinesync/">HubSpot governance checklist</a> come first. Most permission problems we fix are design problems, not tier problems.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="scale">
          <h2>When do limits and scale push you to Enterprise?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Enterprise raises limits on things like pipelines, workflows and custom reporting, which matters for larger teams running several sales motions, regions or brands in one portal. If you are hitting a limit on Professional, first check whether the design is the problem: duplicate workflows and unused pipelines use up limits quickly.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="decide">
          <h2>How do you decide which tier you need?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Design the structure first, then choose the tier that runs it. Write down your objects, pipelines, lifecycle stages, permissions and the reports leadership needs. If that design needs custom objects, a sandbox or finer control, choose Enterprise; if not, Professional set up properly will serve you better than Enterprise set up badly.</p>
